According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global Cement Grinding Aid Market was valued at USD 2.035 billion in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 3.070 billion by 2034, growing at a robust CAGR of 5.03 % during the forecast period (2025–2034). This growth is propelled by the expanding construction footprint across Asia Pacific and Africa, stringent environmental regulations encouraging lower energy manufacturing practices, and the continuous advancement of performance enhancing chemical formulations.
Cement grinding aids are specialized chemical additives introduced during the milling of clinker and raw meal. They lower specific energy consumption, enhance mill throughput, and promote a more uniform particle size distribution. Typical families include organic acids, surfactants, polymers such as polycarboxylates and phosphates, each selected for compatibility with particular cement formulations. Their use not only improves power efficiency but also contributes to consistent product fineness and better overall cement quality.
The market maintains an upward trajectory because construction output expands across Asia Pacific and Africa, where demand for ready mix concrete rises sharply. Furthermore, stricter environmental regulations push producers toward lower energy processes, making additives increasingly attractive. Key players such as BASF SE, Evonik Industries AG and Solvay SA continue investing in R&D; for example, BASF launched a new polycarboxylate based grinding aid in March 2024 aimed at reducing CO₂ emissions per tonne of cement.
